`styled-ppx.generate` orders modules by their compiled imports when it is
given `.cmt` files next to the `.pp.ml` / `.ml` inputs: a module's rules
come after the rules of every module it imports. `Alpha` imports `Zeta`, so
`Zeta`'s rule comes first although `alpha.ml` sorts first by path.

  $ cat > zeta.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-zeta{color:red;}"]
  > let x = 1
  > EOF
  $ cat > alpha.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-alpha{color:blue;}"]
  > let y = Zeta.x
  > EOF
  $ cat > mid.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-mid{color:green;}"]
  > let z = 2
  > EOF
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -c zeta.ml mid.ml alpha.ml

With no `.cmt`, the order is the path order, whatever the order of the
arguments:

  $ styled-ppx.generate zeta.ml alpha.ml mid.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-alpha{color:blue;}
  .css-mid{color:green;}
  .css-zeta{color:red;}

With the `.cmt` files, dependencies come first; modules with no edge
between them keep path order (`mid.ml` before `zeta.ml`, and `Zeta` is
still placed before `Alpha`):

  $ styled-ppx.generate alpha.ml mid.ml zeta.ml alpha.cmt mid.cmt zeta.cmt
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-mid{color:green;}
  .css-zeta{color:red;}
  .css-alpha{color:blue;}

The order does not depend on the order of the arguments:

  $ styled-ppx.generate zeta.cmt zeta.ml alpha.cmt mid.ml mid.cmt alpha.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-mid{color:green;}
  .css-zeta{color:red;}
  .css-alpha{color:blue;}

A `.cmt` belongs to an input when the `.cmt`'s source file (a path from the
directory the compiler ran in, for dune the build context root) is a suffix
of the input's absolute path. A `.cmt` whose source file is not one of the
inputs is ignored: here `other/zeta.cmt` is a different `Zeta`, compiled
from `other/zeta.ml`, which is not an input, so `Alpha` gets no edge from
it.

  $ mkdir other
  $ cat > other/zeta.ml <<EOF
  > let x = 1
  > EOF
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -c other/zeta.ml
  $ styled-ppx.generate alpha.ml mid.ml zeta.ml alpha.cmt mid.cmt other/zeta.cmt
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-alpha{color:blue;}
  .css-mid{color:green;}
  .css-zeta{color:red;}

Two inputs can end with the same source file: `src/foo.ml` and
`packages/x/src/foo.ml` both end with `src/foo.ml`, the source file of
`src/foo.cmt`. Each input takes the `.cmt`s with the longest source file
that its path ends with, so `packages/x/src/foo.ml` takes
`packages/x/src/foo.cmt` and `src/foo.ml` takes `src/foo.cmt`. `src/foo.ml`
imports `Zbar`, so `Zbar`'s rule comes before it:

  $ mkdir -p nested/src nested/packages/x/src
  $ cat > nested/src/foo.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-foo{color:blue;}"]
  > let y = Zbar.x
  > EOF
  $ cat > nested/src/zbar.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-zbar{color:red;}"]
  > let x = 1
  > EOF
  $ cat > nested/packages/x/src/foo.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-xfoo{color:green;}"]
  > let y = 2
  > EOF
  $ (cd nested && ocamlc -bin-annot -c -I src src/zbar.ml src/foo.ml && ocamlc -bin-annot -c packages/x/src/foo.ml)
  $ (cd nested && styled-ppx.generate packages/x/src/foo.ml src/foo.ml src/zbar.ml packages/x/src/foo.cmt src/foo.cmt src/zbar.cmt)
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-xfoo{color:green;}
  .css-zbar{color:red;}
  .css-foo{color:blue;}

When no longer source file decides, one `.cmt` can be the best match of
two inputs. It belongs to neither: it is ignored with a warning that names
both inputs.

  $ (cd nested && styled-ppx.generate packages/x/src/foo.ml src/foo.ml src/zbar.ml src/foo.cmt src/zbar.cmt)
  styled-ppx: src/foo.cmt (compiled from src/foo.ml) matches two inputs, packages/x/src/foo.ml and src/foo.ml; ignoring it
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-xfoo{color:green;}
  .css-foo{color:blue;}
  .css-zbar{color:red;}

Two inputs can define the same module (`copy_files` twins in one sheet).
An import of that module takes the `.cmt` in the importer's own object
directory: `a/alpha.ml` imports `Zeta` and gets an edge to `a/zeta.ml`
only, so it comes before `b/zeta.ml`. An importer with no such module next
to it (`aa/gamma.ml`) gets an edge to every `Zeta`:

  $ mkdir -p twins/a twins/b twins/aa
  $ for d in a b; do printf '[@@@css ".css-%s-zeta{color:red;}"]\nlet x = 1\n' $d > twins/$d/zeta.ml; done
  $ printf '[@@@css ".css-a-alpha{color:blue;}"]\nlet y = Zeta.x\n' > twins/a/alpha.ml
  $ printf '[@@@css ".css-aa-gamma{color:green;}"]\nlet z = Zeta.x\n' > twins/aa/gamma.ml
  $ (cd twins && ocamlc -bin-annot -c -I a a/zeta.ml a/alpha.ml && ocamlc -bin-annot -c b/zeta.ml && ocamlc -bin-annot -c -I a aa/gamma.ml)
  $ (cd twins && styled-ppx.generate a/alpha.ml a/zeta.ml aa/gamma.ml b/zeta.ml a/alpha.cmt a/zeta.cmt aa/gamma.cmt b/zeta.cmt)
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-a-zeta{color:red;}
  .css-a-alpha{color:blue;}
  .css-b-zeta{color:red;}
  .css-aa-gamma{color:green;}

A `.cmt` that cannot be read (for example one written by another OCaml
version) gives a warning, and the modules keep the path order:

  $ echo "not a cmt" > broken.cmt
  $ styled-ppx.generate zeta.ml mid.ml alpha.ml alpha.cmt broken.cmt
  styled-ppx: cannot read broken.cmt as a .cmt file of this OCaml version; keeping the path order of the modules
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-alpha{color:blue;}
  .css-mid{color:green;}
  .css-zeta{color:red;}

A cycle cannot come from one compiler run, but it can from `.cmt` files of
different builds. It is an error that names the modules:

  $ mkdir one two
  $ cat > one/a.ml <<EOF
  > let x = B.x
  > EOF
  $ cat > one/b.ml <<EOF
  > let x = 1
  > EOF
  $ cat > two/a.ml <<EOF
  > let x = 1
  > EOF
  $ cat > two/b.ml <<EOF
  > let x = A.x
  > EOF
  $ (cd one && ocamlc -bin-annot -c b.ml a.ml) && (cd two && ocamlc -bin-annot -c a.ml b.ml)
  $ cat > a.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-a{color:red;}"]
  > EOF
  $ cat > b.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-b{color:red;}"]
  > EOF
  $ styled-ppx.generate a.ml b.ml one/a.cmt two/b.cmt
  styled-ppx: the .cmt imports of these modules form a cycle: A (a.ml), B (b.ml)
  [1]
